Writing an ISO image to a single partition using dd typically doesn't work for creating a bootable, persistent live USB because hybrid ISOs contain their own partition table that needs to be written to the entire device's boot sector, overwriting any pre-existing partition structure. 
To achieve a single-partition USB with persistence after using dd, you must create the persistence file in the remaining free space after flashing the ISO to the entire device (/dev/sdX, not /dev/sdX1).
Here is the correct workflow for a single-partition outcome that includes a persistence file:
Workflow for a Single Partition with Persistence File
This method assumes you have already prepared your live-rw file and configured persistence.conf within it as described previously.
Write the ISO to the entire USB device
Use dd to write the Debian hybrid ISO to the raw device (/dev/sdX), not the partition (/dev/sdX1). This overwrites existing partitions but creates a new, smaller partition structure defined by the ISO itself.
bash
# Identify your USB device first using lsblk or fdisk -l
sudo dd if=/path/to/debian-live.iso of=/dev/sdX bs=4M status=progress
sync # Flush the write buffer
Use code with caution.

Mount the newly created partition
After dd finishes, the USB drive will have a single partition visible (usually /dev/sdX1) which contains the live system files. Unplug and replug the USB stick if it doesn't automatically remount.
Place the live-rw persistence file
Mount the new partition (e.g., to /mnt/usb). Copy the live-rw file you created earlier into the root directory of this mounted partition.
bash
# Mount the USB partition
sudo mount /dev/sdX1 /mnt/usb

# Copy the prepared live-rw file to the root of the USB
sudo cp /path/to/your/prepared/live-rw /mnt/usb/

# Unmount the USB
sudo umount /mnt/usb
Use code with caution.

 
